{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>I am too small for all the kindnesses.</b> From here it is proper for a person to contemplate in his prayer his own deficiency and lowliness, and the mastery of the Master to whom he is a servant — this is what he said, <b>Your servant</b>. And he should contemplate the multitude of the kindnesses of HaShem, blessed be He, and His benefits upon him; and this is what David, peace be upon him, said (Psalms 16:2): ״טובתי בל עליך.״ He says: I contemplate in the ways of HaShem, blessed be He, that He is master over all the world, bestowing upon them benefits — the goodness of the Master upon the servant is not by obligation, but pure kindness; this is ״טובתי בל עליך״ — that is, it is no obligation that should be incumbent upon You. And after he contemplates all this, let him ask his needs. Thus Jacob said: ״Deliver me, I pray.״ And what he said — <b>and all the truth</b> — that is, the justice; and the goodness of the Holy One, blessed be He, and His kindnesses upon a person are not by justice but by kindness. Therefore Onkelos changed the language and did not translate קשוט (truth), but translated ״מכל חסדין ומכל טבוון״ — as though in the Hebrew it were ״מכל חסדים ומכל הטובות״. And the reason Scripture mentioned <b>truth</b> by way of the plain sense (peshat): because the gift of the Land that the Holy One, blessed be He, promised him is in the attribute of truth, for He had already sworn to Abraham concerning this. And this is the plain sense of the verse (Micah 7:20): ״תתן אמת ליעקב חסד לאברהם״ — that is, what You gave to Abraham was kindness, that You gave him the Land and swore to him concerning it. But Jacob — by justice it was his due, for He had already sworn to Abraham." "<b>For with my staff.</b> From here it is obligatory upon a person to remember the days of evil in the time of tranquility, so that he may contemplate his improvement and give thanks to the Holy One, blessed be He, for this. Thus King Solomon, peace be upon him, said (Ecclesiastes 7:14): ״ביום טובה היה בטוב וביום רעה ראה״ — the meaning is: on the day of good, look upon the day of evil."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:17:12.775058741Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}